WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.036
Federico Viticci builds

00:00:01.848 --> 00:00:03.074
his first iOS

00:00:02.897 --> 00:00:03.427
app

00:00:03.966 --> 00:00:05.711
After 45 days and

00:00:05.523 --> 00:00:07.433
530 pull requests

00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:09.131
Federico Viticci is

00:00:08.942 --> 00:00:10.086
nearing TestFlight

00:00:09.898 --> 00:00:10.758
on his first

00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:11.442
iOS app

00:00:11.537 --> 00:00:12.374
and has written

00:00:12.185 --> 00:00:12.940
up his agent

00:00:12.751 --> 00:00:14.434
setup T3 Code

00:00:14.246 --> 00:00:15.720
driving several providers

00:00:15.532 --> 00:00:16.392
across a pool

00:00:16.204 --> 00:00:17.111
of Macs with

00:00:17.029 --> 00:00:19.222
832 GB of RAM

00:00:19.435 --> 00:00:20.661
simulators and a

00:00:20.473 --> 00:00:21.545
fork of pstack

00:00:22.078 --> 00:00:22.620
It is a

00:00:22.432 --> 00:00:23.410
concrete look at

00:00:23.222 --> 00:00:24.460
what a multi-machine

00:00:24.661 --> 00:00:26.323
multi-agent iOS setup

00:00:26.158 --> 00:00:26.865
looks like in

00:00:26.677 --> 00:00:27.419
practice

00:00:27.467 --> 00:00:28.823
including dogfooding

00:00:28.635 --> 00:00:29.719
and bug filing

00:00:29.531 --> 00:00:30.261
once the app

00:00:30.073 --> 00:00:30.721
works

00:00:31.238 --> 00:00:32.157
The post drew

00:00:31.969 --> 00:00:33.490
400 + likes

00:00:33.302 --> 00:00:35.400
and 330k views

00:00:35.934 --> 00:00:36.948
Full story on

00:00:36.759 --> 00:00:37.655
the dashboard
